  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 452 PM EDT FRI SEP 4 2026\n\nAn amplified upper air pattern will be in place to start the long\nterm period, with high pressure over the southern plains ridging\nnorth to Canada and closed lows near the NW and NE corners of the\nCONUS. At the surface, High pressure near the Great Lakes will be\ndropping southeast under northwest flow aloft and supplying us \nwith relatively cooler and drier air compared to recent days. \nEnough lingering low level moisture could be left in southeast KY \nfor a few showers on Sunday before the change in air mass fully \ntakes hold. Dew points look like they'll dip low enough to allow\nfor the coolest valleys to radiate below 60 by Monday morning. \n \nThe upper lows are expected to weaken drift east by Midweek, with\nthe sprawling (but flatter) upper high shifting east to the\nTennessee Valley. Our low level flow will turn to the south and \nsouthwest as the surface high shifts to our east. The low level\nflow and building geopotential heights will give us warmer\ntemperatures and climbing dew points.\n\nThere is model agreement that the upper low initially near the\nnorthwest CONUS will eventually merge with another low traversing\nsoutheast Canada late in the week. This leads to more troughing \nover the eastern CONUS, suppressing the high southwestward. This \nwould allow another cold front to push through. Models show some \nvariation in timing, with the general idea in the latest runs of \nthe GFS and ECMWF being for fropa in the Wed night-Thu time frame \n(perhaps a bit faster than what the required NBM forecast \ncurrently shows). Should it come to fruition, at least modest \ncooling and drying would arrive to end the week.",
